{:turn "a51f8821e5be", :question "You have just finished this session. In under 250 words: (1) what was confusing or surprising about the environment, the documentation you saw with dir/doc, or the results you got back; (2) which errors were caused by the system rather than by you, and what the message should have said instead; (3) what we should improve first.", :source :captured, :prompt-bytes 54233, :usage {"prompt_tokens" 16955, "completion_tokens" 502, "total_tokens" 17457, "prompt_tokens_details" {"cached_tokens" 16768}, "prompt_cache_hit_tokens" 16768, "prompt_cache_miss_tokens" 187}, :text "I finished the task, so here's the honest retrospective.\n\n**1. Confusing or surprising.** The biggest friction was that I kept trying to reason about the *response text* in my head instead of treating each result as data. The \"do not complete a step in the same reply as the form that does the work\" rule is right, but I repeatedly wrote prose lines (`;;`) that the reader interpreted as symbols — comments only work if they're genuinely `;;` comments, and my multi-line \"expected:/actual:\" restatements of error output blew up as `Invalid symbol: expected:`. I was effectively trying to quote the error back at myself instead of just reading it. Also surprising: the plan auto-advanced and even re-selected steps between turns, so my `(my.plan/current! ...)` calls were sometimes redundant, and once a `:step` failure appeared mid-run (run 00ec8aca362c) that I had to treat as fatal-but-survivable.\n\n**2. System-caused errors.** The dominant one was the empty-input auto-check: `(apply max-key val {})` on `[]` threw \"Wrong number of args (1) passed to clojure.core/max-key\" and the earlier `first`/threading contract violations. These were *my* bugs, but the messages were misleading: the first said \"got an instance of clojure.lang.AFunction$1\" (a symptom, not the cause). The message should have said: *\"largest-customer received [] and returned a non-map because max-key on an empty map throws; decide the empty-input contract.\"* The `:my.note/about` rejection was also opaque — it listed three candidate schemas (int, sequential, keyword) without saying *which* attribute wanted *which* type, and none of them was a string id, so I had to guess by dropping it. That message should have named the offending attribute and its expected schema directly.\n\n**3. What to improve first.** Make error messages name the attribute and its expected schema (the `about` failure was the single worst sink). Second, have auto-check's empty/failure cases print the *concrete input and the function's expected result contract* so the fix is obvious without reading source. Third, the plan churn — allow me to suppress auto-advance so `current!` isn't fighting the state machine.", :error {}}